  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1127 PM CST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nSurface observations show a cold front over the Mid-South this\nevening with a mid-level shortwave trough swinging southeast\nthrough the Middle Mississippi River Valley. Only a few, weak\nconvective showers have managed to develop along the cold front\nthis evening. Decreasing instability as the trough makes its way\neast of the region in conjunction with the passage of the cold\nfront will result in no precipitation by Thursday morning. Expect\nlows to remain in the low 50s and upper 40s.\n\nZooming out into Thursday reveals an amplified upper ridge\ndeveloping over the Rockies. Additional shortwaves will develop\nand/or emanate around broader troughing over the eastern CONUS.\nThis will support reinforcing cold fronts to pass through Mid-\nSouth through the latter half of the week, leading to drastically\ncooler temperatures as we approach this weekend. Highs will drop\ninto 50s Friday and will remain at similar levels through the\nweekend. However, morning lows will become impressively cold with\novernight temperatures plummeting into the low 30s starting\nFriday morning. Current NBM probabilities of dropping below 30 F\nsit between 50% - 80% Friday, Saturday, and Sunday mornings,\nhighest in portions of West Tennessee and northeastern\nMississippi.\n\nModels begin to transition the upper pattern into an omega block\nthis weekend, which quickly breaks down as the western trough\nescapes into the southern Plains. Enough lift may manage to reach\nthe Mid-South late Sunday and early Monday morning to produce a\nfew showers south of I-40. This week's successive cold fronts\nwill advect in a deep layer of dry air, which will still be\npresent come early next week. Any precipitation would then\nevaporate, slowly moistening the layers below, but the\nenvironment is not expected to be supportive enough to fully\nbring meaningful precipitation to the surface. Therefore, PoPs\nremain below 30% areawide with nearly no QPF.\n\nEnsembles are still in agreement on the general upper pattern as\nfar out as Wednesday next week, reamplifying the upper ridge over\nthe Plains. Surface high pressure will slide back into the region\nin response, locking more cold and dry weather into the forecast.",
